  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard has two panels, the alert rule is enabled, and I confirmed the panel rename by reading the dashboard back.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has the time field `timestamp`. The HTTP status is in `response`, with `response.keyword` for exact matching.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-7a3a83cc Web logs' (id `s4-7a3a83cc-web-logs-234209968bb8`) has two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of count per `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart of count split by `response.keyword`, top 20 values.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-7a3a83cc 5xx errors' (id `6fbb4eaa-366b-41d0-8965-c769bda4884e`) is an `.es-query` rule on `stackAlerts`.\n   - It runs every 1m and fires when the count is above 10 over a 5m window.\n   - The query matches `response.keyword` against the regexp `5[0-9][0-9]`, which covers 500\u2013599.\n   - It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n4. **Rename:** The response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\". I rebuilt that panel from the same spec, changing only the title, so its query is the same as before.\n   - The rule read-back shows `enabled: true` (status `ok`).\n\nMy choices: I used `response.keyword` as the code field because it is the exact-match field. I used a bar chart for the code panel and a line chart for the time panel. I also made the rule's query a regexp on the keyword field rather than a numeric range, since `response` is a string field.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
